  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1127 PM CDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nTuesday through Saturday:\n\nThe upper level ridge will continue to gradually move eastward into \nmid week. As the high moves away, southwest winds are expected to \nreturn to the state and thus moisture will return. With the ridge \nover the New England states and a cut off low across the desert \nsouthwest, the will be deep moisture returns straight out of the \ngulf. PWAT values will climb across Wisconsin to around 1.5 inches \nby Tuesday night. The WAA will bring temperature back up into the \n70s and could bring some instability back to the Great Lakes Region \nahead of an approaching shortwave. \n\nThe upper level shortwave is expected to move through the Great \nLakes Region Tuesday night into Wednesday morning. Meanwhile the \nassociated sfc low, is expected to develop in the lee of the rockies \nmoving northeast toward Wisconsin. There is still a bit of \nuncertainty in the exact track and strength of this sfc low, \nbut rain chances will increase Tuesday evening hit there peak \naround 60- 80% overnight Tuesday into early Wednesday morning. \nWith the WAA and deep moisture returns, there will be a \npotential for a few rumbles of thunder, but the timing and lack \nof diurnal instability will limit thunderstorm potential. No \nsevere weather is expected. The high PWATs will also mean that \nrainfall could be heavy and there will be a good wetting rain. \nStill a bit too early to really talk about totals, but of the \nglobal ensembles there's a roughly 10-25% chance for an inch of\nrain. This can a will likely change given the uncertainty in \ntrack of the sfc low. \n\nBeyond this and looking ahead toward the end of the work week there \nare additional chances for rain. Global models and ensembles remain \nsplit on how much moisture will return and the speed of the next \nincoming high pressure system. This uncertainty extends up to \nthe mid/upper levels with with the uncertatiny of the timing \nand strength of a 500 mb shortwave and trough. This uncertainty \ncan be most easily seen when looking at the 500 mb cluster \nanalysis. In the mean time there are POPs scattered through the \nrest of the extended (around 50% POPs Thursday becoming 10-20% \nFriday through Saturday). As guidance comes to further agreement\nthose values will change. \n\nPatterson",
